# jellyfin-awards-metadata

## Summary

Generate a complete solution for the `jellyfin-awards-metadata` project. The solution consists of a reusable awards scraping library and a Jellyfin plugin that uses the scraped data to apply award metadata tags to media items.

The primary objective of the first iteration is correctness, maintainability, and extensibility rather than maximum performance.

---

# Solution Structure

The solution should consist of the following projects.

## tmdb-awards-scraper

A reusable .NET library responsible for obtaining, parsing, validating, storing, and exposing awards metadata from TMDB.

This library **owns the awards data model**. Consumers should interact with strongly typed objects rather than parsing JSON directly.

Responsibilities include:

* Discovering supported award types
* Downloading HTML
* Parsing HTML into strongly typed models
* Persisting and loading awards databases
* Import/export of JSON
* Rate limiting
* Retry handling
* Logging
* Data validation

The library should not have any dependency on Jellyfin.

---

## jellyfin-awards-metadata-plugin

A Jellyfin plugin responsible for:

* Plugin configuration
* Scheduled tasks
* Persisting scraper configuration
* Managing awards database
* Applying/removing Jellyfin metadata tags
* Jellyfin-specific logging

The plugin should never parse HTML directly.

---

## Test Projects

Appropriate testing projects should be included.

At minimum:

* Parser unit tests
* HTML fixture tests
* Tag generation tests
* Persistence tests
* Normalization tests

---

# Architecture

The scraper should internally separate responsibilities into independent components.

Example:

Downloader

↓

HTML

↓

Parser

↓

Award Models

↓

Persistence

↓

Consumer

Each component should be individually testable.

---

# Data Model

The scraper library owns the canonical awards model.

The model should represent awards independently from Jellyfin tags.

Example:

* Award Organization
* Ceremony
* Ceremony Year
* Category
* Nominee
* Winner
* TMDB Movie ID
* TMDB Person IDs (when available)

Tags are considered a derived projection of this data.

---

# Persistence

The scraper should expose a persistence abstraction.

Example:

IAwardStore

Implementations may include:

* JSON
* SQLite

The initial implementation may use JSON.

The storage format must include:

* Schema version
* Generated timestamp
* Source information

Example:

* schemaVersion
* generatedUtc
* awards

Future schema migrations should be possible.

---

# Scraper Requirements

The scraper must:

* Allow configurable TMDB endpoint (primarily for testing)
* Use configurable rate limiting
* Support configurable retry count
* Respect Retry-After headers when available
* Support configurable request timeout
* Produce detailed logging
* Discover available award types dynamically
* Support scraping all available award types
* Support scraping individual award types
* Optionally support scraping individual ceremonies/years
* Use TMDB IDs whenever possible
* Expose strongly typed models
* Provide JSON import/export helpers
* Be idempotent

Running the same scrape multiple times without TMDB changes must produce identical results.

---

# Jellyfin Plugin Requirements

The Jellyfin plugin must:

* Be a standard Jellyfin plugin
* Provide a configuration page
* Persist plugin configuration
* Persist awards database
* Expose scheduled tasks

Required scheduled tasks:

* Full awards scrape
* Apply metadata tags
* Remove stale managed tags

Optional:

* Incremental scrape of recent ceremonies

The plugin should not expose the TMDB endpoint unless running in an advanced/debug mode.

---

# Plugin Configuration

The plugin should support configuring:

* Request rate limit
* Retry count
* Request timeout
* Awards storage location
* Award organizations to scrape
* Enable winner tagging
* Enable nominee tagging
* Tag format

The list of award organizations should initially be empty.

A "Query Supported Awards" action should retrieve available organizations from TMDB and persist the discovered list.

---

# Tag Generation

The plugin should support configurable tag formatting.

Default:

award-{awardType}-{awardResult}-{awardCategory}-{awardYear}

Example:

award-academy-awards-winner-best-picture-1999

award-academy-awards-nominee-best-actor-2005

Users should be able to simplify formats, for example:

{awardType}-{awardResult}

Result:

academy-awards-winner

academy-awards-nominee

---

# Normalization

A centralized normalization service should be used for all generated tag values.

Examples:

Academy Awards

↓

academy-awards

Best Original Screenplay

↓

best-original-screenplay

Normalization rules should be shared throughout the solution.

---

# Managed Tags

The plugin should only modify tags that it owns.

Running the plugin must never remove or alter user-created tags.

Generated tags should be tracked so they can be safely updated or removed when:

* Tag format changes
* Awards database changes
* Configuration changes

---

# Logging

Both projects should emit structured logs.

Typical log output should include:

* Award organization
* Ceremony
* Download progress
* Parse progress
* Number of nominations discovered
* Number of tags generated
* Errors
* Retry attempts

---

# Error Handling

Network failures should:

* Retry configurable number of times
* Respect Retry-After when possible
* Continue scraping remaining organizations when appropriate
* Produce actionable log messages

Parser failures should clearly identify:

* Award organization
* Ceremony
* HTML section
* Failed element

---

# Idempotency

All operations must be idempotent.

Examples:

Running a scrape twice should not create duplicate records.

Running Apply Tags multiple times should never create duplicate Jellyfin tags.

Running Remove Managed Tags repeatedly should be safe.

---

# Testing

The scraper should use saved HTML fixtures for parser tests.

Parser tests should not depend on the live TMDB website.

Live integration tests should be optional.

---

# Considerations

Awards data changes infrequently.

Movies within Jellyfin change much more frequently.

Therefore:

* Scraping is expected to occur rarely.
* Tag application may occur much more frequently.
* Performance optimizations are not required for the initial implementation.

Correctness, maintainability, and clean architecture are prioritized over optimization.

---

# Future Enhancements

The architecture should allow future support for:

* Additional awards providers
* Additional metadata sources
* SQLite persistence
* Incremental synchronization
* Person awards
* TV awards
* Custom Jellyfin UI for displaying awards
* Additional metadata beyond tags

The solution should be designed so that adding new providers does not require changes to the Jellyfin plugin.
